THE FROZEN TURN COUNT T

Every reader who wants to reproduce the record in this companion repository will sooner or later need to know exactly what T is, and why it cannot simply be reread from the live system on demand. This section explains both: what the frozen count contains, how it was captured, and why it has to be an archive at all.

T is defined as the number of hub turns that elapsed after the first worker artifact was produced, and it serves as the horizon term of the delegation condition. It is not a stable property of the system; it is a snapshot of a moving figure. The hub's message store is compacted continuously, so superseded turns are evicted and rewritten rather than preserved forever. A count taken against the live store therefore drifts upward as new turns are added and, more importantly, it does not stay comparable to itself, because old rows are not kept the way they were. To put it plainly: if you ran the counting query against the live hub on two different days, you would not be comparing like with like, because the store beneath the query would have been rewritten in between. This is why the paper and the repository do not lean on a live count at all. They rely on a frozen basis, an inert archive taken at a single moment, and they insist that every claim about T be traced back to that archive rather than to whatever the store happens to return today.

To make the drift concrete, the fact sheet gives a recent example. On 14 October 2026 the same query that produced the frozen figure returned 1,046 rows against the frozen count of 343. The difference is not a mystery or an error; it is exactly the expected behaviour of a live counter in a system that compacts its history. The store has grown and been rewritten many times since the freeze, and the number of rows it reports now no longer corresponds to the number of turns that counted toward T at the time the paper was written. Anyone who finds a current figure of 1,046 and imagines it contradicts the frozen 343 is misunderstanding the distinction this section exists to draw. The 343 was not a prediction of how many rows the store would ever contain; it was a carefully capped capture of the turns that were live at one instant, held still so that the delegation condition could be evaluated against a fixed horizon.

The frozen basis itself is a small CSV file in the repository, named data/t_freeze_2026-10-09T0416.csv. Its name carries a timestamp, 2026-10-09 at 04:16, which is the local time at which the capture was made; in UTC that is 00:16 on the same day. The file holds 343 rows, and each row records two things only: a row id and a timestamp. It was taken at commit cd46a7e, authored at that same moment on 2026-10-09. Because an archive of this kind is only as trustworthy as its ability to prove it has not been tampered with or silently altered, the file is accompanied by a checksum. The SHA-256 of the archive is a long fixed string, 5572cf45f6c551739a4e7ed5afabebaf45f40deae28c27b85f0eafcd55eb4258. That digest is the reference against which the archive is verified, and it appears in more than one place so that a reader can confirm the file they are looking at is the same file that was captured.

Verification is not left to trust. The repository ships a script, scripts/verify_t.sh, whose whole job is to take the archive, recount the rows inside it, and check that the count still matches 343 and that the file still hashes to the digest above. In other words, the reader does not have to accept the archive on faith; they can run a single command and confirm both properties for themselves. This is the reproducible path the section is meant to describe: the archive is the ground truth, and the script is the mechanism that lets any reader confirm the ground truth has not moved.

A careful reader will then want to know whether the 343 rows are what they appear to be, because not every row in the store is necessarily a distinct human or automated turn. The fact sheet deals with this directly. Among the 343 rows, 122 of them share one of four second-resolution timestamps, all on 2026-10-08. To be specific: 48 rows share one timestamp, 18 share a second, 26 share a third, and 30 share a fourth. The four shared timestamps, in UTC, are 08:46:41, 14:13:40, 15:21:55 and 17:42:43. Within each burst, the row ids step by two, meaning the ids are not consecutive but increment in pairs. The important observation here is that no model, no matter how fast, emits 48 turns in a single second. Such a cluster of rows all carrying the same timestamp and evenly spaced ids cannot be a genuine run of distinct turns produced in one instant; the only plausible reading is that they are compaction rewrites, rows regenerated by the store's rewriting pass at effectively the same recorded second. This is the reasoning by which these 122 rows are judged to be artifacts of compaction rather than real turns.

Because those rows are artifacts, the repository also supplies a second script, scripts/dedup_t.sh, that removes them so that the reader can see the count with the inferred rewrites stripped out. Running that script leaves 221 distinct turns. So there are two defensible figures to be aware of. The frozen raw count is 343, and that is the number carried into the paper as the basis of T. The deduplicated count, after the inferred compaction rewrites are removed, is 221, and that is the number a reader reaches if they want the count of genuinely distinct turns. The distinction matters because it changes how the delegation condition's horizon should be read, and at the same time it exposes an honest limit of the archive.

That limit is worth stating plainly, because it is precisely where the fact sheet declines to overclaim. The archive carries only two columns per row: the row id and the timestamp. It does not carry the columns that would confirm the compaction inference for certain. In particular, the store has a role column, an active flag, and a finish reason, any of which could settle whether the 122 rows are genuine turns or rewrites. None of those columns is public. They are not exposed, so the inference, however reasonable, cannot be verified directly from the data the repository holds. The repository is therefore honest about the shape of its evidence: the deduplication is an inference grounded in the timing and spacing of the rows, not a fact proven by the archive's own columns. A reader who wants certainty about the compaction interpretation would need access to fields the repository does not publish, and that access is not provided.

Finally, there is the matter of how the frozen figure came to be, and it is a story that also explains why the repository is so insistent on verification. An earlier anchor for the query, the value 277, was carried forward from a reviewer footnote, and it was carried without being verified against the actual store. When two reviewers later re-ran the published query on their own, both of them reached the same result, 266, which did not match the carried figure of 277. This discrepancy is recorded in the repository as incident INC-006. The reader should appreciate what this incident demonstrates: the earlier number was never validated, and the moment it was actually checked, it did not hold. The resolution was a refreeze, and that refreeze is what produced the current archive of 343 on which the paper now relies. In other words, the 343 is not merely the latest number someone happened to record; it is the number that replaced a figure proven unreliable by direct re-running. The whole apparatus of this section, the archive, the hash, the verification script, the deduplication script, and the explicit account of what is and is not knowable from the archive, exists because the repository learned, through INC-006, what happens when a count is passed along without being checked.

That is the complete state of the frozen turn count T. It is a horizon term held in a CSV of 343 rows, timestamped and hashed, with supporting scripts that recount and deduplicate it, an honest statement that the columns that would confirm the deduplication are not public, and a documented history in which an earlier unverified figure was corrected. A reader who wants to reproduce the record should start from the archive, run the verification script, understand why the deduplicated figure differs, and know that 1,046 on the live store does not contradict any of it. What the fact sheet does not include, and what this section therefore cannot supply, is any confirmation of the compaction inference from the store's own non-public columns. If that confirmation is ever needed, it would have to come from access that this repository does not have.

AB-S2-OK
